<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirador Basin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mirador Basin is a lowland area of tropical forest in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It contains a dense cluster of Preclassic Maya cities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-mirador">El Mirador</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakbe">Nakbe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-tintal">El Tintal</a>, linked by raised causeways, which flourished between about 1000 BCE and 150 CE. Much of it lies within protected reserves.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirador-basin.jpg" alt="Mirador Basin" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Authenticmaya · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elmiradortigre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Mirador Basin lies in the remote rainforest of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a>. It is described as two true basins of shallowly sloping terrain dominated by low-lying swamps called bajos, one draining into the San Pedro River and the other into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candelaria-river">Candelaria River</a>. Rugged karstic limestone hills to the east and south enclose a triangular trough of more than 2,169 square kilometres. Whether the landscape is a geological depression at all is disputed: data from NASA's Shuttle Radar Topography Mission show no depression in the area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The major sites of the basin, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-mirador">El Mirador</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakbe">Nakbe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-tintal">El Tintal</a> and Wakna, were first settled mainly in the Middle Preclassic period, about 1000 to 350 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-preclassic">Late Preclassic</a>, about 350 BCE to 150 CE. Because later building was comparatively light, their early remains lie under relatively little overburden from the large constructions of the Classic period (250 to 900 CE). Numerous smaller settlements, such as La Florida, Maaxte, Zacatal, Chan Kan, Tsab Kan, Pedernal, Isla, La Muerta and La Muralla, date mostly to the Classic period.</p><p>Archaeological work over the past two decades has also reached the sites of Xulnal and El Pesquero and the large city of Naachtun in the north-east corner, studied by a team from the University of Calgary. The Mirador Basin Project, directed by Richard Hansen and earlier known as the Regional Archaeological Investigation of the North Petén, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> (RAINPEG) Project, has gathered evidence on the origins and early development of Maya civilisation in the area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The basin belongs to the Maya Biosphere Reserve, the last large area of intact tropical rainforest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. Illegal logging, fires from slash-and-burn farming and the looting of archaeological sites threaten it. Research is carried out with the Guatemalan Institute of Anthropology and History (IDAEH), the Ministry of Culture and Sports and the National Council of Protected Areas (CONAP), and with community organisations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mirador Basin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the following centuries cities grew in the <b>Mirador Basin</b> of northern Petén.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlement at El Mirador expanded from about 600 BCE in the <b>Mirador Basin</b> of northern Guatemala.</blockquote><a href="/state/el-mirador">El Mirador</a></li></ul></section>
